aetheria/characters/player_paperdoll/REA:
# Aetheria — Player paper-doll pack

Everything the engine needs for the hero: one bare **base** sheet per animation plus one
sheet per **equipment slot**, all on the same grid. Equipping a piece is one extra blit.

    player.json        integration descriptor (read this at boot)
    sheets/            42 sprite sheets — 7 animations x (5 layers + baked composite)

## Geometry

* cell 48 x 48 px, authored on a 16 x 16 grid and nearest-upscaled x3
* 8 rows = 8 directions, row order: S SW W NW N NE E SE
* columns = frames, left to right
* pivot (24, 42) — feet contact point
* nearest-neighbour filtering only; never smooth these

## Layer order

    body  ->  armor  ->  shield  ->  helm  ->  sword

`body` is the hero with nothing on: bare skin, hair, linen braies, bare feet. It is drawn
always. Each other layer is drawn only when that slot is filled, at the *same* row and
column — the sheets are pixel-aligned clones of the base, so no offset maths is needed.

## Vanilla canvas

```js
const doll = await fetch('player.json').then(r => r.json());
const img = {}; // path -> HTMLImageElement, preloaded

function drawPlayer(ctx, x, y, anim, dir, frame, equipped) {
  const a = doll.animations[anim];
  const row = doll.directions[dir], col = frame % a.frames;
  const cw = doll.cell.width, ch = doll.cell.height;
  ctx.imageSmoothingEnabled = false;
  for (const layer of doll.layerOrder) {
    if (layer !== doll.base && !equipped[layer]) continue;
    ctx.drawImage(img[a.sheets[layer]],
      col * cw, row * ch, cw, ch,
      x - doll.pivot[0], y - doll.pivot[1], cw, ch);
  }
}

// naked hero:            drawPlayer(ctx, x, y, 'idle', 'S', f, {})
// mail + sword, no helm: drawPlayer(ctx, x, y, 'idle', 'S', f, {armor:1, sword:1})
```

## Phaser 3

```js
// preload
for (const [anim, a] of Object.entries(doll.animations))
  for (const [layer, path] of Object.entries(a.sheets))
    this.load.spritesheet(`player_${anim}_${layer}`, path,
      { frameWidth: 48, frameHeight: 48 });

// create — one animation per layer per direction
const dirRow = doll.directions[dir];
for (const [anim, a] of Object.entries(doll.animations))
  for (const layer of doll.layerOrder)
    this.anims.create({
      key: `${anim}_${layer}_${dir}`,
      frames: this.anims.generateFrameNumbers(`player_${anim}_${layer}`, {
        start: dirRow * a.frames, end: dirRow * a.frames + a.frames - 1
      }),
      frameRate: a.fps, repeat: a.loop ? -1 : 0
    });

// the player is a container of layer sprites playing the same key set
const parts = doll.layerOrder.map(l => this.add.sprite(0, 0, `player_${'idle'}_${l}`));
const hero = this.add.container(x, y, parts);
function equip(slot, on) { parts[doll.layerOrder.indexOf(slot)].setVisible(on); }
```

## Unity / Godot

Slice each sheet by 48 x 48 cells, row = direction, column = frame. Stack one
SpriteRenderer / Sprite2D per layer on the same node with ascending sorting order in
`layerOrder`, share the animation frame index across them, and toggle visibility
per equipped slot. Point filtering, no compression, no mip maps.

## Frame tags

* `attack` — 3-hit combo: right sweep f1, left counter-sweep f4, overhead chop f8
* `charged_attack` — charge f1-6, release f7-8
* `downed_and_revive` — down f2-3, revive f4-5
* `*_composite` sheets are the fully equipped knight, baked; handy for icons and tests

